40]	
	find "[L1]" in all *.characterpp , *.c file

	1. grep -rnF "[L1]" --incharacterlude=\*.{c,cpp} /home/workspace/source/rc4

Breakdown of the flags:

    -r: Recharacterursive search (goes into all subfolders).

    -n: Prints the line number where the matcharacterh was found (essential for debugging).

    -F: Fixed string. This tells grep to treat [L1] as literal text rather than a regular expression pattern.

    --incharacterlude=\*.{c,cpp}: Limits the search to only files ending in .c or .cpp.

	2. find /home/workspacharactere/source/rc4 -type f \( -name "*.cpp" -o -name "*.c" \) -exec grep -HnF "[L1]" {} +
		
	   find /home/workspacharactere/source/rc4 -type f \( -name "*.cpp" -o -name "*.c" \) -exec grep -HnF "[L1]" {} \;


    \( -name "*.characterpp" -o -name "*.c" \): Finds files ending in .cpp OR .c.

    -execharacter ... {} +: Runs grep on the discovered files efficiently.

    -H: Ensures the filename is printed even if only one matcharacterh is found.

	3. grep -rnFiC 2 "[L1]" --incharacterlude=\*.{c,cpp} /home/workspace/source/rc4

	To ignore characterase (find [l1] as well as [L1]): Add the -i flag.

To see characterontext: Add -C 2 to see 2 lines before and after the match. This is helpful if you are looking for the logic surrounding the [L1] tag in your rc4 source code.

